input.new_string:
/* ============================================================
   POSTCARD — the whole card turns over. Front is the photo side
   (stamp, image, caption); back is a real divided-back postcard:
   handwritten message on the left, stamp + postmark + address
   lines on the right.

   The 3D chain is .image-frame (perspective) > .postcard
   (preserve-3d) > two .postcard-face children. Two things here
   are load-bearing:

   1. Don't put overflow (or filter/clip-path) on .postcard — a
      non-visible overflow on the same element that carries
      transform-style:preserve-3d flattens the 3D context and the
      flip degrades to a flat fade. Overflow on an ANCESTOR
      (.page) is fine.
   2. Sizing still comes from the <img> (see .img-el). The FRONT
      face sits in normal flow and drives the card's box; the BACK
      face is absolutely positioned to match it — the same trick
      .placeholder-fill already uses. Don't give .postcard or the
      faces their own width/aspect-ratio, or you reintroduce the
      overflow bug that .img-el's sizing exists to avoid.
   ============================================================ */
.postcard{
  position:relative;
  transform-style:preserve-3d;
  transition:transform .65s cubic-bezier(.2,.75,.2,1);
  cursor:pointer;
}
.image-frame.is-flipped .postcard{ transform:rotateY(180deg); }

.postcard-face{
  background:var(--c-white);
  border-radius:8px;
  -webkit-backface-visibility:hidden;
          backface-visibility:hidden;
}
.postcard-front{
  margin:0;                 /* <figure> default margin */
  padding:12px 12px 0;      /* the matting that used to live on .image-frame */
}
.postcard-back{
  position:absolute; inset:0;
  transform:rotateY(180deg);
